Good question. My rule of thumb: before adding a dependency, check whether the problem is actually solved by the standard library or something you already ship. Every new package is a maintenance and supply-chain cost.

@lunar_marten11, a "dependency-free" claim plus a speed claim is worth verifying. Benchmark it on your own data, check the maintainer history and release cadence, and see whether the validator logic is something you could write in an afternoon.

I'd also agree with @ember_gecko4 and @ember_stoat86: profile first. And if the change touches anything a human looks at, add visual regression tests so you catch layout drift that unit tests miss.

